#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559} :activity/id #uuid "1c5ce268-312c-52a6-9777-1ff390251f96" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180757 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is an affine transformation. The **inverse** of $\\mathbf T$ (if it exists) is an affine transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\\mathbf T^{-1} (\\mathbf T(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf T (\\mathbf T^{-1}(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf u$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf u\\in\\mathbb R^2.$ In other words, the transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ reverses the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\nNot all affine transformations are invertible. So how can we determine whether a given affine transformation is invertible?\n\nTo answer this, recall that an affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ can be expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. We can reverse the action of $\\mathbf T$ by making $\\mathbf u$ the subject of this equation, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ {A}^{-1}\\cdot Au & ={A}^{-1}\\cdot (x-b) \\\\ {I}_{2}\\cdot u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b) \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis equation gives us a method of computing the inverse of an affine transformation. Additionally, we have the following:\n\n*An affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if the matrix $A$ that represents the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is invertible, that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf99caa7-1e47-5fea-87ac-9b91151af127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7697 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35dfb0c0-efab-5193-92eb-6eb22cc2af9f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5"} :step/id #uuid "30275202-8cd8-51e0-a9e1-92aa287dbe42" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180758 :tutorial/content "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf x} = \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf u} + \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf b}.$$\n\nWe wish to determine whether $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ exists and find its matrix form if it does exist.\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get $$\\det(A) = \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1\\cdot (-2) - (-3) \\cdot 1 = 1 \\neq 0.$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTo compute the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ we will use the formula we derived earlier:\n\n$$\\mathbf{u} = A^{-1}(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$$\n\nWe start by calculating the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix form of the inverse affine transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4c88a3e-6514-5b56-ab23-306164b0a3a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7698 :tutorial/title "Finding the Inverse of an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "6802d261-21db-5122-886a-1dda8f3a0db4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4"} :step/id #uuid "3f8aecda-4d54-5528-939f-886f4ab93593" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb"} :step/id #uuid "21d1cffb-5a12-5ee7-ad2a-181e2a8d4474" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4"} :step/id #uuid "35abe35f-9e29-539b-aaa5-a2546fd2fbfb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180761 :tutorial/content "Consider the square in the $uv$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8cc0923da86a1a1ac6b666893397263ef3865448080500caa6f17510547d901.png)\n\nWe wish to explore the image of this square under an affine transformation $$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},$$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors.\n\nSimilar to linear transformations, there are three possibilities depending on the value of $\\det(A)$.\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular ($\\det(A)\\neq 0$). In this case, our square is mapped to a parallelogram.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane, we get the following parallelogram:\n\n![](images/36/36da067393fa53f4296373d65e3c6177a54d1a901824188687b4a74ea171afdf.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ has at least one nonzero entry. In this case, our square is mapped to a line segment.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following line segment:\n\n![](images/5e/5e5481e5b2f2218a6097020e260dc991cde05574372c724aa07527458257d94c.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ is the zero matrix. In this case, our square is mapped to a point on the plane.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following point.\n\n![](images/00/0072c5e06674f79f82e771744fda217e6558830f7a6e4ab387aa9dbf4dda09bc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "375d6591-08dd-5dbf-b36a-28013839a28b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7706 :tutorial/title "Image Types Formed by Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "5ec4662b-6870-532f-83f9-f7a9cbe5504e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180762 :tutorial/content "In general, if we consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the mapping\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, then we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases: \n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane (namely, the point with position vector $\\mathbf b$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "560907bc-354a-5693-a8df-e6824454755d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7710 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Image Types"} :step/id #uuid "d71cf118-7383-5aea-a47c-8359fc7dffe4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179860} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406970} :activity/id #uuid "13211b98-9cdf-5456-a254-0f2d03a88cbb" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633 :question/id #uuid "08a12b2e-6f73-5fc0-987c-f4d58e5bc21f"} :step/id #uuid "1d9d1d09-adca-5f60-87c8-6eda1712954f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968 :question/id #uuid "353e50fd-8521-51a5-ae98-273dc887e6d8"} :step/id #uuid "57752ad6-299b-53a6-80c6-38f70d7331f1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089 :question/id #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5845-8a74-564fa863b598"} :step/id #uuid "8adcf20b-9143-567f-8916-5a2f8686af25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406969 :question/id #uuid "e92cb69b-a679-593b-8882-9cab8d26a8fb"} :step/id #uuid "b7332e33-ee2f-5908-9f85-c258b5d80b5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406972}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649 :question/id #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8"} :step/id #uuid "c564b67d-ab6c-5a8c-9962-63cefc9e226d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406973}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406955} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412072} :activity/id #uuid "5dadfabb-9118-5878-8b7b-abe67ab51d1a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968 :question/id #uuid "353e50fd-8521-51a5-ae98-273dc887e6d8"} :step/id #uuid "57a42c1b-28d8-57a5-acfd-dd199e88f044"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414 :question/id #uuid "0a795336-4ab0-5101-bbca-aa94f4ca2a93"} :step/id #uuid "5d41d9ac-1e31-5132-8e1e-f51eb5e05b80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659 :question/id #uuid "6d452c80-d748-5af8-bbed-4f41e3a6ab8b"} :step/id #uuid "7e79ca9f-a884-5f7c-a928-03d9e0201c9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649 :question/id #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8"} :step/id #uuid "d9eae084-1f11-516b-9c30-618279f69755" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995 :question/id #uuid "24203ad0-c666-584c-ace2-35e7e7f927ec"} :step/id #uuid "e391a44c-ebaa-5dea-a1f8-4caf64dd590c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412071}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412067} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435192} :activity/id #uuid "fe41318c-94a2-58c6-a22a-4e621881d521" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259634 :question/id #uuid "8b41b10b-145c-5e96-a5f6-3186f40b3311"} :step/id #uuid "1b672460-44db-5bc9-bbbb-d13ca12e5373" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435193}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259625 :question/id #uuid "c388fe2c-958c-51fb-bcca-cd64f6bc965e"} :step/id #uuid "55933838-8190-5662-8692-814c8a886119" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435191}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659 :question/id #uuid "6d452c80-d748-5af8-bbed-4f41e3a6ab8b"} :step/id #uuid "7c2cc732-641c-57e5-95b3-542bab8f0800"}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435190} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445237} :activity/id #uuid "8d550678-9055-5adb-a6ff-5b7c64c11448" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259660 :question/id #uuid "2c660cc6-fa1c-504a-b604-d474fb35da3e"} :step/id #uuid "1e5edc5f-97f4-5214-8e0a-72cb196631dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089 :question/id #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5845-8a74-564fa863b598"} :step/id #uuid "31efd4db-96ca-5899-a79c-9be17347f5b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259626 :question/id #uuid "51a6b16d-03c2-54f1-bf60-c27ba81220b5"} :step/id #uuid "320920d1-2f71-59ff-899e-943cde1b5fe3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259651 :question/id #uuid "ab7706f1-5139-5e95-9482-c097bef51185"} :step/id #uuid "33e812b7-0a19-5479-b824-948d87ff1605" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445235}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389257 :question/id #uuid "b31d6cdc-6f82-507f-838b-9eddf7b3f11a"} :step/id #uuid "86b72ad4-07e7-5b0a-8c51-311053773e89"}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445234}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179431 :question/id #uuid "f9d3291b-b95b-536b-93ec-9815f7f554d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12428" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v - 2, \\qquad y = v - u + 5.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix representing the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](images/b3/b3d58dc3ce18b0f5c977b930088152bb7b9c4c821edb656bc478904b67ae8acf.png)\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v-2 \\\\ v-u+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/83/83207d393e930561d8d1f45fb0b21a61f0af7350bfc684f38e846a2cebd1493b.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/01/01afe0c9e10a71665dc2ea7d4bd076bb2b9ba63e8dff10d6fbd3264a88008c39.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\det(A) = 0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb68de75-4474-546d-86b8-f138d3e02e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ and $T_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177622} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed7a6a6-8520-54a7-839e-cdc981fe0e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177623} {:answer/id #uuid "4480be1c-4540-5e18-aec8-3414727ed2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177624} {:answer/id #uuid "47a455ed-a11c-5fd3-8140-40b883244967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177625} {:answer/id #uuid "498db3d4-987b-56f1-a074-e73e8096d60c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177626}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0520a840-c83e-595e-9e75-bfd39bf1ffc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177621} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157a2168-9826-565a-8830-f23a709d7bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378485} {:answer/id #uuid "2b00227f-3181-5c81-833c-c343685c4e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378486} {:answer/id #uuid "4139bbf5-cb92-5a61-bdcf-80d6e60d8d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378487} {:answer/id #uuid "62dce562-3e92-5a70-ade6-fe2dcc38ed52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378488} {:answer/id #uuid "0e21f2b4-91b5-55f3-8ba2-c09c67989eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378488} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f1fe27d-d0a0-5f02-86d9-789a27697fbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378484}] :question/id #uuid "c4e42d76-39ef-52d6-9aa7-90e3d7d61fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124109" :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v-u \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u-2 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v-1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-u \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u+v \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=-2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-2 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 2-6\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfe983a6-86f9-5e85-b458-f781e21c140d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448110} {:answer/id #uuid "0a98043d-5dff-5ea4-9e03-3d50d77054b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448110} :answer-field/id #uuid "41004b63-e782-515c-b8db-25e0f62efb36" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449095} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb4ef7fd-13ca-58f5-a761-f38a0e1a271e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448112} {:answer/id #uuid "800c874a-5daf-5173-b771-501d14f8edfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448112} :answer-field/id #uuid "d532ade3-90d1-5f1e-9570-f9069f48be56" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449096} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a595b56c-93c8-5082-873d-5f72788d8c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448114} {:answer/id #uuid "02c905a9-96d8-56f3-b13d-9f2cb1327c36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448115} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff9bbb31-cd77-53c4-9780-2f910cb04323" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449097}] :question/id #uuid "267c4ee8-beb7-545b-be5a-57b42fce6778" :question/math-academy-id "q-124111" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $det(A)=0$\n- **III.** The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339e8e64-9356-52e6-b8e5-4bc0a3bef99d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257238} {:answer/id #uuid "64e1df3e-a7f1-5dc7-a307-f5d4104c7ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257667} {:answer/id #uuid "83389cc9-94ff-5bc4-b38c-6f000223c647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257943} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fc735b-362f-5cbb-b6ef-45b3c94f8a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258339} {:answer/id #uuid "cde11d91-78bf-5001-82d0-1c36acaf93ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257667} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ce2651-e7b5-5d4c-82da-81b59ed6577a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259402}] :question/id #uuid "72479cb8-eb92-5b20-8e4e-e8b5aa32f2b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124210" :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}5u-2v-5 \\\\ 10u-4v+1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u-v-4 \\\\ u+5v+7\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2u+4v-2 \\\\ 3u+6v+3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u-2v-5 \\\\ 10u-4v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u-2v \\\\ 10u-4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{vmatrix}=-4\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot 10=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v-4 \\\\ u+5v+7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}=5\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1=6\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+4v-2 \\\\ 3u+6v+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+4v \\\\ 3u+6v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}=6\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{2}$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34bfe153-da06-5e91-af3f-09f4545070dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448128} {:answer/id #uuid "3edad985-cc26-5a5e-af3e-6700e60c71ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448128} :answer-field/id #uuid "eadb4e27-873a-5521-888f-69f05d97b5e2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449104} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba40652-c044-55c4-8a94-35b541239ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448130} {:answer/id #uuid "947b203f-00c8-5eca-95c1-efce409088a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448131} :answer-field/id #uuid "00944724-9959-5392-a192-85f20e1ec85e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449105} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20afd0a3-b0c0-5791-b565-fc7e5b831524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448132} {:answer/id #uuid "9dda385e-2665-5c6a-93bd-f8b119850f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448133} :answer-field/id #uuid "36adaa95-128f-5bfd-b7a0-fecb70c1d1da" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449106}] :question/id #uuid "3230d0ef-3334-5e9c-bc28-426c4388c6b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124213" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-6v-2,\\,y=5v-u+9.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $det(A)=4$\n- **III.** The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-6v-2 \\\\ 5v-u+9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-6v \\\\ -u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}=5\\cdot 2-(-6)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1daaab98-cbe8-5352-bacb-b5473639f7d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448134} {:answer/id #uuid "61d8fcde-639e-51e8-ad9a-369bc099832e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448135} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8bf1e46-3b2c-510e-8fc9-4a0cac9760b0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441c54c9-8ecc-5a18-9ccb-3f4010752b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448136} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4e6a4b-8eb3-565f-a9a9-d81923448b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448136} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf33e0a-cba5-5662-8a21-99a57c476012" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449108} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb140b7e-c87d-5f72-9d09-3e36fca1601a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448138} {:answer/id #uuid "e8389ee7-d6c3-5f91-8889-ed2eee89b8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448138} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb21737e-2666-5ebe-87e7-f10521798b5a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449109}] :question/id #uuid "67b5699e-cde1-5170-92cc-4d8a2c62f657" :question/math-academy-id "q-124215" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=8u-6v+2,\\,y=4u-3v-1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $det(A)=1$\n- **III.** The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8u-6v+2 \\\\ 4u-3v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8u-6v \\\\ 4u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}=(-3)\\cdot 8-(-6)\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d8644aa-30c8-5f84-95f8-591ae101f520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448140} {:answer/id #uuid "7d56c5c8-75e5-5ea5-96ad-cda45dad43a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448140} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e36209e-566c-514f-b7e4-aaee36041867" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea2f9e42-7bf6-5f40-9368-3dda1b852ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448142} {:answer/id #uuid "73d58032-ea3b-55f4-8461-e52c44559040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448143} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e9de89-bea2-5bee-8fc7-7e275041411e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449111} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c502c020-a561-562e-9709-4015d249e451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448144} {:answer/id #uuid "e3628c3d-6abc-5c9d-9e38-4a53a7f3c693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448144} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90de5ac-5ad0-5d21-bc0b-30dee157da82" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449112}] :question/id #uuid "d8f35619-da30-55db-920a-269c9cc6d2af" :question/math-academy-id "q-124219" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+2v-5,\\,y=2u+v+6.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $det(A)=0$\n- **III.** The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+2v-5 \\\\ 2u+v+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5edab00-2da3-5cc1-baaf-38a53c01b46a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378492} {:answer/id #uuid "17516c76-c109-51fe-8509-d6e4daba333f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378493} {:answer/id #uuid "33ce05dd-c97f-568c-a254-6549100a83da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378494} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4162f2-f683-51d9-86f0-4efbfa8bbc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378495} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd307f0-6f73-523c-a203-cdb8babd11f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378496} :answer-field/id #uuid "24f97229-b8b0-5ac5-8717-387f1cee4f89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378491}] :question/id #uuid "ad1b6191-80b0-5b35-b5bb-b0b81838cf26" :question/math-academy-id "q-124207" :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v+6 \\\\ 7v-2u+6\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2u+v-2 \\\\ 4+4u+2v\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+9 \\\\ 8u+7v+6\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v+6 \\\\ 7v-2u+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ -2u+7v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{vmatrix}=7\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2)=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+v-2 \\\\ 4+4u+2v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+v \\\\ 4u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+9 \\\\ 8u+7v+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ 8u+7v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}=7\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 8=7.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{3}$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1c40089-8dc1-5f8e-99cd-67b7a73633a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378499} {:answer/id #uuid "166a688e-3a46-5593-af42-a3a0030830cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378500} {:answer/id #uuid "51e512c9-c419-5d08-b0c6-1853fae86196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378501} {:answer/id #uuid "3f86dd95-0eef-5d67-99d2-4aafce89a663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378502} {:answer/id #uuid "79162ec6-2663-5699-8e94-17de1a8e8962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378501} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe045b69-03e8-5e78-b803-d6d4b2202a0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378498}] :question/id #uuid "9105f94c-01af-5534-a486-823fdcf9cd79" :question/math-academy-id "q-124208" :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}6u+3v+3 \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}1-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v-2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v-7\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+3v+3 \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+3v \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{vmatrix}=5\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 10=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=4\\cdot (-3)-(-4)\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v-7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}=7\\cdot (-6)-(-4)\\cdot 8=-10\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{3}$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{3}$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b2486ad-4110-5df8-bbd8-25b1073349f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378506} {:answer/id #uuid "1097ce9c-f0c1-5872-9aa5-6d9ef401ce5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378507} {:answer/id #uuid "0575605a-616f-5d19-a3d9-3c12944fcf43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378508} {:answer/id #uuid "a583a229-5168-5661-85e5-c39cb5ba12c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378509} {:answer/id #uuid "cd398182-4f35-5456-bf06-390f361a2596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378507} :answer-field/id #uuid "81091068-b7f8-57eb-bb1a-9c7cdd84e5e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378505}] :question/id #uuid "fa12f762-cadd-562d-b233-d0acc6275ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124209" :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-2u-5v-9 \\\\ 4u+9v-9\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+3v+4 \\\\ 3u+3v-1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+v \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2u-5v-9 \\\\ 4u+9v-9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2u-5v \\\\ 4u+9v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{vmatrix}=9\\cdot (-2)-(-5)\\cdot 4=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+3v+4 \\\\ 3u+3v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+3v \\\\ 3u+3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+v \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+v \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179432 :question/id #uuid "af48b8ba-874a-5753-9874-a2b13fc5a65b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12429" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n![](images/6c/6cce99dca6804d7a15dbf107312e367d45c2e9ef917e7f212fba56a018b6ae5f.png)\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is \n\n![](images/3b/3b195dda0cddb83bb414ff40ec076286765b7f599e1bac8cc7d04bdd6e9273e2.png)" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $\\mathbf T$ gives us the following information:\n\n![](images/ea/ead1edec5d6fa82de8eb0137a838826221e0f45093782bc1b0a32111b05a37f4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 7-(-9)\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = -7 + 3 + 8 = 4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0086e1d8-f0ee-53c6-8443-35f56170d9d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256787} {:answer/id #uuid "0d67fff4-252d-5baa-b919-6e2e71d71094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256912} {:answer/id #uuid "80d9c10d-d8ce-5ef8-9f6d-73c22d791bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257926} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f2b7b2-0f28-5166-96d2-007456d5faee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258602} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a8a822-04f8-5fb6-8bb9-09b853fcf229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258602} :answer-field/id #uuid "79824dff-905f-590c-af91-7c9745e11bfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259237}] :question/id #uuid "24203ad0-c666-584c-ace2-35e7e7f927ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124117" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-2+1+(-8)=-9.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2c8010-5295-5660-a90e-af4378d39d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257024} {:answer/id #uuid "950db2a3-3a74-5937-9dc1-4c4b5b8fa96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258091} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a42389-75b5-52cf-b7a5-c644378ae742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258343} {:answer/id #uuid "b55f9bcc-fe18-553b-bcd2-5f45f3b48c99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258356} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ec479e-7861-5034-961b-84aecd6de1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258091} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1c8a8a6-99b7-5fe6-a134-274d8fb51189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259401}] :question/id #uuid "b5f8edc9-279a-5eac-9e38-88ca67abf13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124249" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 \\\\ b & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7-(-5)\\cdot (4)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+4+3=10.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709e3e28-43f0-5774-9967-f2a77296b518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257772} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5307ee-7945-53c6-8b5c-e3c7b40d7f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257864} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a72ca3-839f-596e-9ba3-db4dee4a50f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258642} {:answer/id #uuid "deb6b58c-00ac-51db-9e70-4039e8ace72e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258721} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ff2522-63f2-5ecd-8947-0cdfd4cb6b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258721} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed49f22-b423-5b9a-b990-ba7eb27d6825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259480}] :question/id #uuid "c388fe2c-958c-51fb-bcca-cd64f6bc965e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124224" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ a & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1\\cdot (-9)-2\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+(-9)+20=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a8b2e4c-71f4-525e-882c-30ff8c65e625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257299} {:answer/id #uuid "6713e9ff-c15a-5839-bf29-fa79fff395dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257695} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6d52a8-e329-53f2-9