uch that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bfb243-3f51-50bb-a40d-8bd7b816d5c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518993} {:answer/id #uuid "5c76486d-0cd9-5267-b109-d5ac0d8c7703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519130} {:answer/id #uuid "6b14d34e-024d-5c98-a7cd-041c6930d3b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519149} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c17d5d-2d75-57cc-95a7-828178d428fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519254} {:answer/id #uuid "e90c6a79-88fe-5546-8423-a7c541bc4746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518993} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe3cf3ad-3ee5-5331-8b54-4ce6e5fd60bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519510}] :question/id #uuid "f2f205d5-62a6-56a8-a8b2-ff79782e487c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94003" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171832 :question/id #uuid "7d2dc7ea-947d-5cda-8c95-c28281ecd792" :question/math-academy-id "e-8158" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff3e6c7ad9b4c8aa8e45f374a284c0f55a4655acab5ce77650e5e5eab8a3eea7.svg)\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D=P^T \\! A P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=5\\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/7e/7e2e5d56fa414820a4a21b54c1c382e822da079044416e35d511f75a8b4a4a3f.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/69/69886ad4b83344964ea9341595b201aaf8725d805afc0b71036413708ecbe1bf.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}+8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/4d/4d63a240ce9979a10db9b4b339c5e66387f030b7434e6fe544d32b1a122cb94d.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/f6/f604278ad5afd886a82142b3511ca042a7a1568c2dabb901ae7fb117508c2953.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/54/54e0d9e7d7cd07271dfc6a6c3439a7c394e5bf3d85f0630dd1c6f9889561f2c0.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6386cba4-6bef-5ce6-a28f-7756865697af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555222} {:answer/id #uuid "87b92cc8-999d-5cf3-bf8b-bdd8e08a2989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555316} {:answer/id #uuid "88323906-807d-5c80-975d-3c3a60ac5954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555318} {:answer/id #uuid "a5853336-65bd-51a4-8d09-1814205711bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555395} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ca4907-7b6a-5cd0-ad7e-2e9a14158a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555222} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf95aab-26ad-5d4c-96ff-37b37822372e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171303}] :question/id #uuid "c7e02b11-9226-5c5b-9fda-bf26ea5fa1e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-93602" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83034cba-0c66-5f4a-b7ab-09a5c1a7fcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404627} {:answer/id #uuid "febd8376-fe28-5c27-8963-ff102a571340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404628} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6af10c-5fa3-59e2-8df1-756aa2dce145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404629} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5de280-13f5-504f-9445-8833d1c988ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404630} {:answer/id #uuid "69324513-aecc-5af8-be4a-19ff3184bb08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404627} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f41eb2e-7fb3-52bc-a716-8a62499f8e67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404626}] :question/id #uuid "f738fa7a-a5ce-518d-9ada-841b441fb202" :question/math-academy-id "q-94056" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ea1678-0464-5847-b42f-729bf0e8d8e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269735} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd1622e-6ab5-5f78-9167-ee025db03178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270247} {:answer/id #uuid "901f840a-a58d-5541-b4cc-78f9ec3f395b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270525} {:answer/id #uuid "be31c22b-ddcc-5f2d-95df-0a26ebb9e765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270948} {:answer/id #uuid "e036c742-0e1e-577c-b9ec-563f3052514f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269735} :answer-field/id #uuid "a995ec0b-d934-5185-b9f2-a89cc802ee30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271817}] :question/id #uuid "d04c9e9d-40ba-57d1-8646-0a16510ea6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140828" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336cf6f9-9c54-5e2f-ada9-732ce949d970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355117} {:answer/id #uuid "90130aba-f333-517d-a251-9c6490022f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355937} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e02ec3-be4b-5fc9-ba0e-33f5cdf90cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356411} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1e47ff-c344-58bd-8036-cba9d9d844b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356427} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ed1e82-f4d6-50f9-916e-71cbc3f03b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356427} :answer-field/id #uuid "5552efef-2a50-51c7-b3f0-40453df1c5da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357063}] :question/id #uuid "beb606d5-93cc-5df7-ad46-456782d7ef97" :question/math-academy-id "q-94053" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca90f25-8a2a-5154-8abc-8f1caaac1737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355048} {:answer/id #uuid "4e874988-4b8c-5650-b515-a02bdcf8db24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355364} {:answer/id #uuid "5180d0c3-657e-5b75-ac2b-25066a4ea962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355390} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb170a0-8ac1-5736-bef7-d6a15eeabe0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356341} {:answer/id #uuid "d73f5496-3e39-5d24-8f7b-c48a9b96fdc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356341} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda65562-ed2d-5933-b1e5-85c35db9bffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357315}] :question/id #uuid "b031f1a5-d4c2-57ba-adf6-a3fa295e4440" :question/math-academy-id "q-94054" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=6\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-4$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56f0e8db-19ca-5e1a-acef-433c78628572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364511} {:answer/id #uuid "1823a197-4084-5668-86ee-6f6631856c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364512} {:answer/id #uuid "f00dd1c5-3fcc-5dff-bacb-b531e82059c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364513} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d58908-b478-5a13-939f-a731e544060f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364514} {:answer/id #uuid "8cee7f48-d011-59dc-ac39-3664c1537063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364512} :answer-field/id #uuid "537c6748-dc18-5328-8608-350f3c43bf42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364510}] :question/id #uuid "987ecebc-d297-5962-b8d0-90f351ffca4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94043" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-10$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-10)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-10)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56bc7cf9-dd1b-5316-add9-231be19d109f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364518} {:answer/id #uuid "b27a1670-2d5b-5bba-92a6-f0a17c0ce246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364519} {:answer/id #uuid "881bcb91-1f0d-5fd7-97a5-86876b601912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364520} {:answer/id #uuid "d84e1a54-ac78-5b2a-a104-f35ae3afdc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364521} {:answer/id #uuid "db75b88c-c492-572e-968f-86146a9098af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364520} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd4411f-b5f0-53e2-9b19-b5941f836d1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364517}] :question/id #uuid "7cf8708f-5209-58e9-a19a-fa6e74f6f1a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94044" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404612 :question/answer-fi