eld/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404613}] :question/id #uuid "976b5be4-8521-548d-a6b3-f77ad28ed1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-94041" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c224e271-277d-5f1a-9cc2-c5f3c1736629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{10} & -\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404621} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e4a6c1-e440-5739-9e5b-4073535092ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404622} {:answer/id #uuid "51ae4b95-ac89-5527-8195-692cf68f4405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404623} {:answer/id #uuid "5537d4ab-5df2-5c89-8196-ce1e4ae511b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404624} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2a3f76-d0f1-5a60-8575-903fd51dffb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404625} :answer-field/id #uuid "eac5900c-9744-5a1f-9961-900e2ff573d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404620}] :question/id #uuid "46cbbf2d-cd23-5aab-9cf1-375dd44b0200" :question/math-academy-id "q-94046" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e29233-e0dc-59a9-8b71-89547fc06293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491564} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8be98c-dbfa-5fb0-9905-3ffacd2e675f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} & \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491601} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbc487c-5094-5d5f-8c80-f2cbf38f3c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491728} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cf2940-8c8f-50ee-bc73-4ae0c1be7870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491740} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb57498-4f30-5829-bbc1-a63f82ea49d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491826} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc785a6c-6e3c-5d7b-a2af-c97237d81ba3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491971}] :question/id #uuid "23817e7f-6a06-51ca-bb89-ae0fdb8c7004" :question/math-academy-id "q-94049" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a706c372-7bf9-519b-bf47-eec640055a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555398} {:answer/id #uuid "c0eaea37-d763-51e1-b779-86ad5cab32fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555463} {:answer/id #uuid "d5816e57-b424-539b-bcae-e9c26275321d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555507} {:answer/id #uuid "f73191f0-64c8-52a9-bb54-a9dff6856396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555612} {:answer/id #uuid "fc03ffd9-3aab-5745-80b2-933e00a5c3a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555627} :answer-field/id #uuid "6125238e-cd95-58be-8f9d-65d56d196e45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555685}] :question/id #uuid "f4374452-85e0-5d4e-85af-02cf21a24ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94051" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=9\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171833 :question/id #uuid "fd780d94-dd75-5a57-b34d-4bbaffcb95ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8164" :question/problem "![](images/71/71ae5f07963d6fc4006ac682fb99d9654957564b0238494aa5d6f2c6ab3a1161.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/c0/c037ac599b0f26311ef49cea15a50b3c215f5f66af6677cc400470e36ac1dc12.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/e8/e89397c2eb8ac9affa119178536b827fe4c9f710c5569dbdc5138c3d6498a413.svg)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/cb/cbbbb9019a6fe09c63e1a69478321553144988117e35d9947336c964841b9cfc.svg)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/ea/ea9a9bf2fc7a7328202006234fa0288237b663ae31c382cc0e98f301eb3999ae.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb6f98c-b45e-537c-a457-1f57056e9e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519042} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae566ac-fe5f-5c58-a6f9-26a40344d78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519184} {:answer/id #uuid "90548aa5-959a-5095-a1ec-969585e26d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519230} {:answer/id #uuid "de84cd40-30b1-5bdf-bf92-d43dfaf0b310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519359} {:answer/id #uuid "e5262114-a0da-5731-a575-c6db872f7c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519184} :answer-field/id #uuid "567b4465-6b20-53a1-be8a-d9caef043ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171297}] :question/id #uuid "22c685a9-35f7-5e7f-8909-4579d1ac4e02" :question/math-academy-id "q-93598" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afbd007-f1e6-5e55-8cbe-4bcfee30c365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491507} {:answer/id #uuid "4a38eb59-71d6-57ae-b362-18321880abad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491595} {:answer/id #uuid "6b57765b-b3fc-58da-ae16-8958c804d7b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491646} {:answer/id #uuid "6c66f93f-55cb-5fc9-800c-1394bd01e241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491649} {:answer/id #uuid "7873082b-15f9-5724-b785-35989be3f136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491595} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4fd8bc-59fc-526b-b28d-cb3ea2256c56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171315}] :question/id #uuid "6397dc6a-e670-5a27-9059-a00e14d9ffee" :question/math-academy-id "q-94024" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532bfb59-affa-5c62-a8e7-358e36a9bc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270008} {:answer/id #uuid "aea60c28-5e87-5191-952b-3b00f7cb6adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270821} {:answer/id #uuid "c179c9da-d404-5cbd-968a-2220b6fba57e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270984} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3ecd7b-55a3-5ef3-ad46-ad378f716f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271197} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6dc910-3586-5cbb-930a-6f920df14dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270821} :answer-field/id #uuid "34f87073-a16a-52a8-8a68-7c23a2ba2530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271610}] :question/id #uuid "b6cb0f9e-f73e-53e5-b7a2-f0d1aeb7047d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140825" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11094d7d-473b-5600-bfc5-733545716858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354818} {:answer/id #uuid "5b631e09-e722-569e-9d36-b5e5bd11a9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355472} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ed70c1-bd6a-5f64-8476-7de7bb357815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356104} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b354c7-b1a9-56a6-a163-87b30aa9b2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356141} {:answer/id #uuid "b526304e-6636-525a-941a-b5b2567221f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356141} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cb265d-6ef7-5d0d-ad97-2153f283ebbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357272}] :question/id #uuid "aac4543d-11c8-50e7-9d8c-0d3017badc72" :question/math-academy-id "q-94035" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1383bfc2-4360-5709-a0a8-f10b27226553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354836} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf62f53-4cca-53fd-9904-3006d9e5e4c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354914} {:answer/id #uuid "6c84ca9d-2a09-535e-ac82-9783439e70e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355622} {:answer/id #uuid "8f61224e-9396-5066-9c8e-bd0d738416d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355931} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c07977-b2ba-5f8d-bb10-ef441705d5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355931} :answer-field/id #uuid "110fa06e-fdc4-596b-8460-688e417bf995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356947}] :question/id #uuid "5fd6de92-b207-5bcd-bc32-b23f271f69c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94039" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Computing $A-(-5)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc11a993-8cf5-5223-b3b9-b70e311d1658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364504} {:answer/id #uuid "79c91797-739a-5d6a-bcae-7e9304578d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364505} {:answer/id #uuid "05292e3d-b7ef-50d0-ac06-9e15cf43a97c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364506} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc2c84e-14f9-539b-9995-48bacb1cea77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364507} {:answer/id #uuid "0c471abc-770a-5695-81c4-c7fea640c8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364506} :answer-field/id #uuid "1987f539-91e2-50d5-a960-a7dc5210dea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364503}] :question/id #uuid "de6d89ac-ee2b-5c2e-9894-edbf9a3d9bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-94034" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d49197c-8ea0-5037-8658-b8800ba11d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394882} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb14de4-81a2-5a22-9b22-a9c795e5d80a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394883} {:answer/id #uuid "2d287e51-ff4c-5d64-aaf3-306e594af48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394884} {:answer/id #uuid "298cf117-73a5-5dba-8a49-4651e4dc4bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394885} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a2c500-01a4-5141-91a3-878c1f2e4f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394884} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa1f9db6-ad26-5de3-b141-6cab0e8d74e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394881}] :question/id #uuid "bc9e75e4-89e6-5c5c-b897-fce34f0588db" :question/math-academy-id "q-94026" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $-4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "262152dc-d095-58ad-b8fd-54192e4a8aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394889} {:answer/id #uuid "8249bee5-05ff-5f60-bc29-4262dfab7c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394890} {:answer/id #uuid "f0637a74-e581-54c5-ba07-1d64511750e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394891} {:answer/id #uuid "495e9dc8-57a2-539e-ba4f-277d6dcd3829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394892} {:answer/id #uuid "e0694bd3-785b-543e-9d91-a341c5a4399e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394889} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca689d19-127e-5288-a0b5-11c9bff01ef0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394888}] :question/id #uuid "e266e00f-9fa3-5a93-849a-c0c7dd5a8f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-94032" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4661f467-2570-50d1-8ea7-764925bd6483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394896} {:answer/id #uuid "e507e2a2-00b8-50ad-bb5f-c430ff99f487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394897} {:answer/id #uuid "6b92abc9-a55a-50fc-ab02-ea6cb5653f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394898} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc656d3-5ba8-5298-bf61-43af779427a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394899} {:answer/id #uuid "13967d9f-3611-5bfe-bb71-32016afcadc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394896} :answer-field/id #uuid "7113dc5c-6527-51ab-8e9c-b39f83dfb5dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394895}] :question/id #uuid "b5923242-1a47-5487-a3a8-4c37dcb336a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94037" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ Computing $A-(-9)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+9I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}+9\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "349a333f-7c5a-5481-82b0-60cb63d7e507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491558} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3fb012-e655-54c0-b54f-afddf4c20499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491644} {:answer/id #uuid "94417bd7-eb48-5c39-9406-4f41780ccd10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491707} {:answer/id #uuid "c96bbaba-0f0d-5501-965f-1c3ab78bf9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491796} {:answer/id #uuid "fa236c72-29cd-50bc-b16a-514c5b7d0eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491880} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f366c1-707a-566c-ae94-1cc85de0d171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491981}] :question/id #uuid "71c26421-0b8a-5150-b5ca-ed9ed8ecdd75" :question/math-academy-id "q-94038" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ Computing $A-(-6)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af7ba2c-f995-5cdb-b7ee-cabe8c1cbeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491506} {:answer/id #uuid "3e19570d-c86d-5aef-8ce6-081272d7624d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491576} {:answer/id #uuid "b134fbf7-d5b6-5f41-ab04-2a7e561d26d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491763} {:answer/id #uuid "b82e7bd9-f08e-549b-a5ee-19697ab81504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491773} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c9344d-89ec-5291-9170-aea2d82d9332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491763} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14357fc-2106-5858-9b65-b92159c22d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491955}] :question/id #uuid "8da40f3d-5d18-5923-96f9-268309e6cbba" :question/math-academy-id "q-94029" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c48aa5-451f-5b58-9fdf-d8472558f09b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540593} {:answer/id #uuid "4abf62fc-86eb-5564-b600-98ba0a148dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540661} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8a67c4-b331-5d37-8678-fa4fd7b2a874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540689} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc39615-cf6d-548a-a9c6-d33a2dc2a0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540738} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4de4cf-7e82-5f05-9f5b-966287d3a5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540871} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6527b4b-120d-5aff-a1f0-c36c16a9c694" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541035}] :question/id #uuid "6ce9a2b1-181d-53a4-9a85-7b3316309f0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140823" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$ Computing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3335 :topic/title "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120621} :activity/id #uuid "1cf102a8-b50b-50b9-b9e1-ba6d06bcd005" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121018 :tutorial/content "Sometimes there are lots of calculations involved in finding the determinant of a matrix using a cofactor expansion across the first row. \n\nFortunately, however, we don't always have to use the first row! We can use *any* row we like. A cofactor expansion across any general row is known as a **Laplace expansion**.\n\nAcross the row $i,$ the Laplace expansion of an $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{i1}{C}_{i1}+{a}_{i2}{C}_{i2}+⋯+{a}_{in}{C}_{in},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}C_{ij}}$ is the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ in $A.$ Remember the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{black}M_{ij}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}M_{ij}}$ is the minor of the entry.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the determinant of the matrix above, it is most convenient to use a Laplace expansion along the row with the most zeros. So, let's use Laplace expansion across the third row ($i=3$) as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6e8d5f7f4111e8963a6d4c7daf2bd5116d8099e18f41c53f2dae73f659f01e35.svg)\n\nSo, we reduced a $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "e9ee666a-da25-5521-b0bf-5d90a89b6e5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3001 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cd1eda6c-ca73-5cc1-91b6-a6fdef05cb05" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1637e23-6566-5e45-af4c-b27333f882d1"} :step/id #uuid "c97ef0ce-8fe0-5462-8edf-ea6b6fce37f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121019 :tutorial/content "We can also use the Laplace expansion across any *column* to find the determinant. Across the column $j,$ the Laplace expansion is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{1j}{C}_{1j}+{a}_{2j}{C}_{2j}+⋯+{a}_{nj}{C}_{nj},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}C_{ij}$ is the cofactor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{ij}}.$\n\nIn other words, the Laplace expansion across a row or column is the sum of the entries in the row or column multiplied by their respective cofactors.\n\nTo find the determinant of a matrix, we can choose to use the Laplace expansion across any row or column, so choose the row or column that has the most zeros, to cut down on the calculations!" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c3bdfc6-9f31-558b-9e75-725b2a92027f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3002 :tutorial/title "Laplace Expansion Across a Column"} :step/id #uuid "678f4e20-a38c-51ea-909d-5d7d1577436d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6f57f3-d9f0-5ea7-a557-e729a8c0777f"} :step/id #uuid "bb444283-a8b6-5668-8558-32d154a2839b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a88f6712-2115-55ce-abe4-b3c27e2e105f"} :step/id #uuid "ada1363c-ec10-55ea-a2b2-e872dcb901ad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120622}}] :activity/title "Finding Determinants Using Laplace Expansions" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119992} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425355} :activity/id #uuid "3deab7c7-601d-5aa7-a706-f09271ec407c" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425352 :question/id #uuid "cd765ff8-911d-50bf-a599-8cf7e9df87e6"} :step/id #uuid "0b0ff6e1-7449-568f-b593-44cc09974a71" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338752 :question/id #uuid "fa886c44-6fbc-583a-a5aa-8d0e7dbb66ff"} :step/id #uuid "37d0a7e2-99c5-5b88-87e2-cc62f6bed604" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345342 :question/id #uuid "82b9ddc1-7a19-5213-af13-fb4eb5bd3ace"} :step/id #uuid "4caaadf2-991c-5e83-b9c9-f302cb53937f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265821 :question/id #uuid "ba53d6e2-edac-5757-8fc8-0a9f2f226683"} :step/id #uuid "5c12a418-c9d4-5275-a573-e37841dac7e1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425353 :question/id #uuid "f2f19b4d-d8c8-5ecf-9fc4-030f5c14f82f"} :step/id #uuid "6f37f61b-9c5a-5f69-89ee-bb85a710c786" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425354}}] :activity/title "Finding Determinants Using Laplace Expansions" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425339}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048505 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119587 :question/id #uuid "8235213e-4e88-57f6-89b0-de589ad2b38d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4418" :question/problem "Calculate\n![](images/15/15738eec7022be44c90577b931f2e8aa8446a8fc3f911260fcf468ae6ddd2d57.png)" :question/worked-solution "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3 \\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-8)+(0-0) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/ce/ce96c053abb756977f717e7130257af39307e5f663596042ddcb5b14b0153cb8.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1637e23-6566-5e45-af4c-b27333f882d1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2645148c-8f0c-59d8-8328-daf1c11c002e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324280} {:answer/id #uuid "8423c962-04ae-56a6-a351-28d4870327a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325084} {:answer/id #uuid "8cbab88f-65b0-53c1-8212-e69a999b2288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325165} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed70273-c8e9-55fc-8960-e486a7629dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325182} {:answer/id #uuid "d57a2363-d584-51bb-988e-accd88178c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324280} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a39b12-5834-56dc-bd92-b12e1a44915e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326386}] :question/id #uuid "00bb680c-6e72-5c8f-931f-0ff9d17c1cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-42095" :question/problem "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "The $3$rd row of the matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+1\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{31}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1-2)-2(2-4) \\\\  & =-1+4 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+(-4)\\cdot {C}_{22}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4(2+3)+2(1+6) \\\\  & =-20+14 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 3-(-6)=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "819d8586-5eea-5e34-b50c-421d163d1a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118665} {:answer/id #uuid "0cad95a9-c08b-5a7d-bc34-f5eef8e5c070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118666} {:answer/id #uuid "479e9e2e-e06e-5031-94a1-c70bace159ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118667} {:answer/id #uuid "5b11b433-8691-5ee4-b8ed-85da94b9d41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118668} {:answer/id #uuid "683b14ae-9a2d-592d-a551-ef9ad96ef2fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118669}] :answer-field/id #uuid "99cee47c-f5d8-5093-8004-e516e2380918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118664}] :question/id #uuid "1c3e37f2-7f08-5c72-b37d-bf0e0953e983" :question/math-academy-id "q-42105" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd row:\n$\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08629f1e-c5d2-5f81-a5e9-5c9e466b1f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336102} {:answer/id #uuid "18da98e4-38dc-51ab-83d5-b67960d79dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336265} {:answer/id #uuid "214a22fa-c0d4-5880-a3b5-7190b59080b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336343} {:answer/id #uuid "7644a866-0487-5361-bb17-5ef5e9a87fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337097} {:answer/id #uuid "f148a18a-cb53-5e47-a600-44fc81b1522e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338144} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c5f631-3543-5c10-a4d1-d4d6fb576326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338713}] :question/id #uuid "ad21c558-ae5a-5895-ba39-892c87b1ad75" :question/math-academy-id "q-70108" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $1$st row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+1\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 5=15.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b353273-335f-5780-afb0-be75dcae7075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336283} {:answer/id #uuid "2d120b04-e055-5b9e-9000-4eb3aaf2ce01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336459} {:answer/id #uuid "40d52219-9a22-5340-9d52-4fb05d2cbce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336640} {:answer/id #uuid "484a4e8b-6df4-53f0-bacc-40a4d9dbcd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336702} {:answer/id #uuid "67b19c38-9273-5308-a318-07e5af5d7a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336958} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbaf0ba2-3df2-59a1-a7fc-b27211f32d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338725}] :question/id #uuid "6b33eca9-2a94-5443-8eea-bc482a963164" :question/math-academy-id "q-70109" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{21}+0\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(9-2) \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=-4\\cdot (-14)=56.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000e1d96-8935-52bc-b473-92fb6b2a631c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336029} {:answer/id #uuid "66ca18d6-9c9c-5fe7-81e8-baffadbc3312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336949} {:answer/id #uuid "784268b3-b15a-510d-b906-29f354e1b2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337114} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4e359d-2c20-5e25-a85b-2c50b2ca090a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337144} {:answer/id #uuid "e28197d9-4a50-5427-a602-17b7dcaf663c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337114} :answer-field/id #uuid "496c21df-6120-50a3-8f23-74c46cc96ee2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338407}] :question/id #uuid "e26acf58-9de8-5792-a31a-d5cce9613711" :question/math-academy-id "q-70128" :question/problem "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "The $2$nd row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23}+1\\cdot {C}_{24} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{2+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+2\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(6-2)-(-6-2) \\\\  & =-8-(-8) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+1\\cdot {C}_{12}+2\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(2)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-6-2)+2(0-6) \\\\  & =-(-8)+2\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =8-12 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|=-0+(-4)=-4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ac0f97-eeb8-5783-936b-1cb59033a9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336679} {:answer/id #uuid "4b236f4f-c6e5-553f-9345-4b6a8bba7c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336725} {:answer/id #uuid "6029e681-c7c2-5a8b-96eb-6ad30cbe83ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336903} {:answer/id #uuid "72576609-7b8d-50eb-8ffa-8aef7ffc7d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337056} {:answer/id #uuid "862a7584-abcb-5134-9117-8e928e486ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336679} :answer-field/id #uuid "907db199-a612-5898-a313-2594bace9c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338542}] :question/id #uuid "fa886c44-6fbc-583a-a5aa-8d0e7dbb66ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-70133" :question/problem "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "The $4$th row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+3\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{21}+2\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2-3)+2(1-3) \\\\  & =-5+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+1\\cdot {C}_{32}+1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-2)+(-2+1) \\\\  & =-2+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot (-9)+3\\cdot (-3)=18-9=9.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703f38f5-d562-5737-a3e9-455a97a42f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343204} {:answer/id #uuid "917467d4-68ad-589a-b372-c2656ddfbe56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343511} {:answer/id #uuid "95733d44-1f56-5609-980e-fc36d7c34c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343545} {:answer/id #uuid "b84505eb-59e1-5ba6-848a-5b83870be4a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343855} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9247e7-0c47-5cc3-ac67-d7bc0bbe7d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343511} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb3dcc7-ea2d-577b-bed7-1da5dd43872a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344884}] :question/id #uuid "7219fa5b-320d-5221-9146-e95094c6f719" :question/math-academy-id "q-77487" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $1$st row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the first $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{21}+5\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+5|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-3-0)+5(-6-4) \\\\  & =3-50 \\\\  & =-47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second  $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+1\\cdot {C}_{22}+5\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|-5|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-2)-5(-8-4) \\\\  & =-2+60 \\\\  & =58\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|=-47+58=11.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63c78bb2-c05e-5042-99b4-4ba26257cfd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343085} {:answer/id #uuid "ac08282d-ec1c-52a7-bb55-b48b1164c66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343752} {:answer/id #uuid "b45e6f2e-a5c6-545c-b716-1f84c527b6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343821} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e13b1e-923e-54b7-9baf-180717ccf5c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343831} {:answer/id #uuid "b86e0c96-da3a-5204-a16d-eb611c571f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343085} :answer-field/id #uuid "818d92fb-dffb-55cf-8415-a01be38b7c20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344721}] :question/id #uuid "942da52d-05d9-58c3-b707-f2f2db2a2ed1" :question/math-academy-id "q-77491" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+2\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{24} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-15-32) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-47) \\\\  & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 47=94.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc79991-58e0-5122-ac80-577fb9452fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342358} {:answer/id #uuid "39027aaf-7f10-521d-9d79-b04232289b93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342707} {:answer/id #uuid "56f2432c-2ff0-5b6c-bc53-3d21355bba2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342962} {:answer/id #uuid "a136c0cd-cd24-579c-9993-2a92eec516cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343660} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b55ff0-0c21-55e1-ac05-eb2cb572eea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342358} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fd7b9a7-034b-59d4-8409-d4980260d202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344548}] :question/id #uuid "ca7d486d-765a-531d-b071-af032848aae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-77800" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+0\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23}+3\\cdot {C}_{24} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-7-0 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=3(-7)=-21.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6835c8-2cfb-5a8c-acb9-0d6f35bdff99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342497} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5eeff0-d8f4-5176-9a11-2cfb5f801710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343013} {:answer/id #uuid "764c4dcd-d2a0-53ec-a5f4-c09bd26aca40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343261} {:answer/id #uuid "983ffe27-13e6-5f16-8ab7-7b68417a6e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343572} {:answer/id #uuid "a090828d-2b1a-51fa-8296-142970b0c8f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343572} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f7b556e-bf75-5353-a507-76bc077cec3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344628}] :question/id #uuid "b9e41934-b6da-5af2-884b-ea417e91c9f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-77802" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =-2\\cdot {C}_{41}+5\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the first $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{11}+1\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 4\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-4-7)-(-8+2) \\\\  & =11+6 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second  $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{11}+1\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 4\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4-7)-(0+0) \\\\  & =-11-0 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=2(17)+5(-11)=-21.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a5c820-be5b-5082-926b-e68028730b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369688} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea3bb4d-945d-58f9-9adb-63f707e063fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369689} {:answer/id #uuid "81a658e1-0516-5466-a149-e2eb4f1f4c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369690} {:answer/id #uuid "f056f334-c31b-5405-870a-03cf05d84686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369691} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5e7563-f924-5173-b2ac-acb8371abc72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369692} :answer-field/id #uuid "f92affe9-b7e7-55a2-9d79-b25b03dbdff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369687}] :question/id #uuid "b87ab7d2-a978-564c-941d-810575e01d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-130035" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the third row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot 16=-32.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6067ab7f-666f-51fe-a3ad-de2b5d607d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370935} {:answer/id #uuid "c031e32c-214f-5b2c-8394-eab613b8d9d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370936} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe9381e-b0ef-5210-b4ea-f2615e9433da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370937} {:answer/id #uuid "7d661da1-7cdc-5100-b6d8-e9dfbca11fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370938} {:answer/id #uuid "e88af25c-0464-5342-b56b-d091bcd4dd1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370939} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5bc926-a168-5bf8-9c60-65f432035bc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370934}] :question/id #uuid "fe1a4bd6-0ca7-5877-a7c4-a06ff6754131" :question/math-academy-id "q-42106" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the third row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot 6=-12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4136cbe3-0e51-5894-9db2-0b5720c6937d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371079} {:answer/id #uuid "9675a04a-b132-5d93-b85b-92a4e236cb0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371080} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca29f97-3356-53c7-aca4-9390ad0dc517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371081} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6c6e57-06f4-5117-bb87-036736e8cd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371082} {:answer/id #uuid "3c10fb02-e2c8-5f7e-bf76-33ecc7934fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371082} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ca3f24d-5ce2-5c3a-aa5b-7f7ec9ca8da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371078}] :question/id #uuid "92efbe12-f093-58f1-a6e2-e6fbb71066d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-77486" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+1\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{21}+0\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(16-(-2)) \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+1\\cdot {C}_{32}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-4)-2(2-2) \\\\  & =4+0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=-3\\cdot (-36)+1\\cdot 4=112.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f4bae87-8e07-5dc7-9219-6f715b2ead18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425341} {:answer/id #uuid "67de8883-fc93-5260-a637-aad0f0e00eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425343} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad5be32-123d-550b-a8af-5eb97cc44261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425344} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bfe935-3937-5ddc-93c2-d9d7115d3c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425346} {:answer/id #uuid "eec09744-a423-5762-9061-2c0d2667e27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425349} :answer-field/id #uuid "836f09e9-0332-59b3-a2c7-f5f742bda069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425350}] :question/id #uuid "f2f19b4d-d8c8-5ecf-9fc4-030f5c14f82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-77803" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+4\\cdot {C}_{33}+3\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the first $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{22}+(-4)\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(6-0)+4(0-2) \\\\  & =-12-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second  $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+-1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6+2)-(-6-0) \\\\  & =-4+6 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=4(-20)-3(2)=-86.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Row"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119588 :question/id #uuid "6f0a6499-03db-5833-b175-4835325d34d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4419" :question/problem "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n![](images/82/82b151cbc7d19f4afa375763d0ca8eb281d76e11f2d775c068e97dbf3d40cae4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{42} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/ef/efb849ac6e240be4d0a6aac3e7f9405158b8b1bb996956b7346c02b5bee751b1.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6f57f3-d9f0-5ea7-a557-e729a8c0777f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7088fd-91ec-5f65-a3a0-66351d709298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118539} {:answer/id #uuid "d73bd2c1-7553-53b7-8bb0-d54e6a1c3e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118540} {:answer/id #uuid "126eb8c8-5841-57fc-a8c4-e7c7a7c3d686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118541} {:answer/id #uuid "1286544d-d34a-50f5-8bb5-3bbcfc10eed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118542} {:answer/id #uuid "25cb3a8a-32ea-5429-bff7-3c8afd6cb5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118543}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ec8331-3600-5e02-8b08-885aea3977a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118538}] :question/id #uuid "ca38aeec-dd7c-5ece-9b79-dcabf88c42fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-36224" :question/problem "What is $\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f59107-a020-5bc9-8690-e29324d1f3ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118653} {:answer/id #uuid "91dfed8b-74d7-5b7a-970a-d72ecb2057cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/